{"id":"security-cloud-native-security","corpus":"k8s","title":"Cloud Native Security and Kubernetes","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/cloud-native-security/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Cloud Native Security and Kubernetes"},{"kind":"p","text":"Concepts for keeping your cloud native workload secure."},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes is based on a cloud native architecture and draws on advice from the CNCF about good practices for cloud native information security."},{"kind":"p","text":"Read on for an overview of how Kubernetes is designed to help you deploy a secure cloud native platform."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Cloud native information security"},{"kind":"p","text":"The CNCF white paper on cloud native security defines security controls and practices that are appropriate to different lifecycle phases."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Develop lifecycle phase"},{"kind":"li","text":"Ensure the integrity of development environments."},{"kind":"li","text":"Design applications following good practices for information security, appropriate for your context."},{"kind":"li","text":"Consider end user security as part of solution design."},{"kind":"p","text":"To achieve this, you can:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Adopt an architecture, such as zero trust, that minimizes attack surfaces, even for internal threats."},{"kind":"li","text":"Define a code review process that considers security concerns."},{"kind":"li","text":"Build a threat model of your system or application that identifies trust boundaries. Use that threat model to identify risks and determine how to treat them."},{"kind":"li","text":"Incorporate advanced security automation, such as fuzzing and security chaos engineering, where it's justified."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Distribute lifecycle phase"},{"kind":"li","text":"Ensure the security of the supply chain for container images you execute."},{"kind":"li","text":"Ensure the security of the supply chain for the cluster and other components that execute your application. For example, this might include an external database that your cloud native application uses for persistence."},{"kind":"p","text":"To achieve this, you can:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Scan container images and other artifacts for known vulnerabilities."},{"kind":"li","text":"Ensure that software distribution uses encryption in transit, with a chain of trust for the software source."},{"kind":"li","text":"Adopt and follow processes to update dependencies when updates are available, especially in response to security announcements."},{"kind":"li","text":"Use validation mechanisms such as digital certificates for supply chain assurance."},{"kind":"li","text":"Subscribe to feeds and other mechanisms to alert you to security risks."},{"kind":"li","text":"Restrict access to artifacts. Place container images in a private registry that only allows authorized clients to pull images."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Deploy lifecycle phase"},{"kind":"p","text":"Ensure appropriate restrictions on what can be deployed, who can deploy it, and where it can be deployed. You can enforce measures from the distribute phase, such as verifying the cryptographic identity of container image artifacts."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can deploy different applications and cluster components into different namespaces. Containers and namespaces both provide isolation mechanisms that are relevant to information security."},{"kind":"p","text":"When you deploy Kubernetes, you also set the foundation for your applications' runtime environment: a Kubernetes cluster (or multiple clusters). That infrastructure must provide the security guarantees that higher layers expect."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Runtime lifecycle phase"},{"kind":"p","text":"The Runtime phase comprises three critical areas: access, compute, and storage."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Runtime protection: access"},{"kind":"p","text":"The Kubernetes API is what makes your cluster work. Protecting this API is key to providing effective cluster security."},{"kind":"p","text":"Other pages in the Kubernetes documentation have more detail about how to set up specific aspects of access control. The security checklist provides suggested basic checks for your cluster."},{"kind":"p","text":"Beyond that, securing your cluster means implementing effective authentication and authorization for API access. Use ServiceAccounts to provide and manage security identities for workloads and cluster components."},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes uses TLS to protect API traffic; make sure to deploy the cluster using TLS (including for traffic between nodes and the control plane) and protect the encryption keys. If you use Kubernetes' own API for CertificateSigningRequests, pay special attention to restricting misuse there."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Runtime protection: compute"},{"kind":"p","text":"Containers provide two things: isolation between applications and a mechanism to combine those isolated applications to run on the same host computer. Those two aspects—isolation and aggregation—mean that runtime security involves identifying trade-offs and finding an appropriate balance."},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes relies on a container runtime to set up and run containers. The Kubernetes project does not recommend a specific container runtime, and you should make sure that the runtime(s) you choose meet your information security needs."},{"kind":"p","text":"To protect your compute at runtime, you can:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Enforce Pod Security Standards for applications to help ensure they run with only the necessary privileges."},{"kind":"li","text":"Run a specialized operating system on your nodes that is designed specifically for running containerized workloads. This is typically based on a read-only operating system (immutable image) that provides only the services essential for running containers. Container-specific operating systems help isolate system components and present a reduced attack surface in case of a container escape."}],"lemmas":{"clouds":["cloud","clouds"],"clouding":["clouding","cloud"],"clouded":["clouded","cloud"],"natives":["native","natives"],"securities":["security","securities"],"kubernetes":["kubernetes","kubernete"],"concepts":["concept","concepts"],"keeping":["keeping","keep"],"keepings":["keeping","keepings"],"keeps":["keep","keeps"],"kept":["kept","keep"],"secures":["secures","secure"],"secured":["secured","secure"],"based":["based","base"],"baseds":["baseds","based"],"bases":["base","bases"],"basing":["basing","base"],"architectures":["architecture","architectures"],"draws":["draw","draws"],"drawing":["drawing","draw"],"drawn":["drawn","draw"],"drew":["drew","draw"],"goods":["good","goods"],"best":["best","good"],"better":["better","good"],"practices":["practice","practices"],"practiced":["practiced","practice"],"practicing":["practicing","practice"],"reads":["read","reads"],"reading":["reading","read"],"overviews":["overview","overviews"],"designed":["designed","design"],"designeds":["designeds","designed"],"designs":["design","designs"],"designing":["designing","design"],"helps":["help","helps"],"helping":["helping","help"],"helped":["helped","help"],"deploys":["deploys","deploy"],"deploying":["deploying","deploy"],"platforms":["platform","platforms"],"whites":["white","whites"],"papers":["paper","papers"],"papering":["papering","paper"],"defines":["defines","define"],"defining":["defining","define"],"defined":["defined","define"],"controls":["control","controls"],"controlled":["controlled","control"],"controlling":["controlling","control"],"appropriates":["appropriates","appropriate"],"appropriating":["appropriating","appropriate"],"appropriated":["appropriated","appropriate"],"differents":["differents","different"],"lifecycles":["lifecycles","lifecycle"],"phases":["phase","phases"],"phasing":["phasing","phase"],"phased"